    "result_text": "The build is green. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What I did\n\nThe spec required exposing `namesakeCount` \u2014 the number of existing owners sharing the same first and last name (case-insensitively) at create time \u2014 and reading it back via `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\nI added it as a new, cohesive unit rather than growing any existing function:\n\n1. **New pipeline function** `AssignNamesakeCount.java` \u2014 a single-method class (WMC_other = 0) that counts existing namesakes via `ownerRepository.findAll()` and calls `owner.setNamesakeCount(...)`. It runs before `save`, so `findAll()` sees only pre-existing owners \u2014 exactly the \"before this create\" semantics.\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted an `assignNamesakeCount` step between `assignCustomerCode` and `save`, keeping the same `govern: [ transaction ]` wiring.\n\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `namesake_count` column with a plain getter/setter (each CC = 1), so the value survives to the `GET` read. MapStruct auto-maps it into `OwnerDto` by name \u2014 no mapper change needed.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `namesakeCount` integer to the `Owner` schema so the generated DTO carries the field.\n\n5. **Schema/data** \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column to all four `schema.sql` files, and appended the value to the hsqldb `data.sql` positional owner inserts (the only data file kept in positional sync; h2/postgres use column lists, mysql was already positional-independent).\n\nThe design keeps structural impact low: the counting logic lives entirely in a fresh single-method class (no surrounding complexity to hold), and the only touches to the large `Owner` class are trivial CC-1 accessors. The `AcceptanceTest` and full suite pass green.",
